// Monophonic pitch detection for sysl: YIN, in plain time-domain arithmetic, generic over the float
// width — and the high-pass filter and note arithmetic a tuner wraps around it.
//
// This is the block a consumer writes to reach it:
//
//     dependencies {
//       pitch { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/pitch", version = "0.1.1" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate names the package, `pitch`; the module a program imports is `sh.sysl.pitch`. It
// declares no dependencies of its own -- everything it needs is the standard library's `sysl.math`.
// In particular it does not depend on `fft`: the difference function is summed directly, which is
// what a microcontroller can afford at a tuner's frame sizes.

package {
  name    = "pitch"
  version = "0.1.1"

  // The compiler this was written and tested against. The least common thing it leans on is a
  // default read at a generic parameter's type -- `a4: F = 440.0` -- so an older floor would need
  // that probed rather than assumed.
  sysl = "0.0.158"
}

// **`heap = true`, and it is asked for once.** `detector` sizes the buffer the difference function is
// written into from the lowest frequency, which is only known when it is called, and `highpass` sizes
// its state from the number of sections. After that, `detect` and `process` allocate nothing, frame
// after frame -- which is what a program on a board needs of them.
//
// **What is NOT here is `os` or `posix`.** Nothing in this package opens, reads or writes anything:
// samples come in as a slice, and where they came from is the caller's business.
requires {
  heap = true
}
